<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bois Caïman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1791">1791</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Bois Caïman ceremony</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bois Caïman, Gator Wood, near Le Cap in the French colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, was the site of the meeting of enslaved Africans in August 1791 at which the first major slave insurrection of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a> was planned. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou">Vodou</a> ceremony, attended by 200 enslaved Africans according to a witness, was presided over by the houngan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutty-boukman">Dutty Boukman</a> and the mambo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecile-fatiman">Cécile Fatiman</a>, and is remembered above all for the sacrifice of a black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> pig. Within days the Northern Plain was in flames; the French later captured and beheaded Boukman. No first-hand written account survives and some scholars doubt that it took place, but it became a symbol of resistance to oppression.</p></div>
